    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an expanded version with a higher concentration of technical terms:\n\nResearchers have developed novel LNP formulations that exploit branched-tail architectures to augment thermal stability in mRNA-based therapeutics, counteracting the inherent degradation propensity of negatively charged mRNA and encumbered charge partitioning. By replacing standard ionizable lipids with polymeric branched tails (e.g., poly(PAMAM-OH)), these modified particles exhibit an elevated transition temperature (Tm), facilitating a slower degradation rate of PCR-cleaved mRNA via 5' → 3' RNase H-mediated hydrolysis and mitigating premature release from the LNP shell via cleavage at the lipid-nucleic acid interface. Concomitantly, the incorporation of cholesterol derivatives with high saturated methyl ester levels (Lันทrcode) enhances the structural integrity of the nanoparticle shell by modulating membrane fluidity and inducing defined hydrophobic interactions with the lipid bilayer, thus countering oxidative stress-induced denaturation of the encapsulated nucleic acid and subsequent destabilization of the complex. These advances suggest a promising pathway toward shelf-stable mRNA products that can be stored at 4°C without refrigeration, thereby obviating the need for ultra-low temperature freezers.",
      "source": "target",
